\fs70 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li200\fc0\cf0 Class Cluster Description\

\fs14 \

\f0\b0\fs28\li1500 NSAttributedString
\i  
\i0 objects manage character strings and associated sets of attributes (for example, font and kerning) that apply to individual characters or ranges of characters in the string. An association of characters and their attributes is called an 
\i attributed string
\i0 . The cluster's two public classes, NSAttributedString and NSMutableAttributedString, declare the programmatic interface for read-only attributed strings and modifiable attributed strings, respectively. The Foundation Kit defines the basic functionality for attributed strings, while the remainder is defined here in the Application Kit. The Application Kit also uses a subclass of NSMutableAttributedString, called NSTextStorage, to provide the storage for NeXT's extended text-handling system.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2300\f3\b\fs24\fi-800\li2300\fc0\cf0 Note:	
\f0\b0\fs28 NSAttributedString is 
\i not
\i0  a subclass of NSString. It contains a string object to which it applies attributes. This protects users of attributed strings from ambiguities caused by the semantic differences between simple and attributed string. For example, equality can't be simply defined between an NSString and an attributed string.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Because of the nature of class clusters, attributed string objects are not actual instances of the NSAttributedString or NSMutableAttributedString classes, but are instances of one of their private concrete subclasses. Although an attributed string object's class is private, its interface is public, as declared by these abstract superclasses, NSAttributedString and NSMutableAttributedString. The attributed string classes adopt the NSCopying and NSMutableCopying protocols, making it convenient to convert an attributed string from one type to the other.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 You create an NSAttributedString object from scratch by using one of 
\b initWithString:
\b0 , 
\b initWithString:attributes:
\b0 , or 
\b initWithAttributedString:
\b0 . These methods initialize an attributed string with data you provide. You can also create an attributed string from RTF data using 
\b initWithRTF:documentAttributes:
\b0 , 
\b initWithRTFD:documentAttributes:
\b0 , 
\b initWithRTFDFileWrapper:documentAttributes:
\b0 , or 
\b initWithPath:documentAttributes:
\b0 . See ªRTF Document Attributesº for more details on reading RTF.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 An attributed string provides basic access to its contents with the 
\b string
\b0  and 
\b attributesAtIndex:effectiveRange:
\b0  methods, which yield characters and attributes, respectively. These two primitive methods are used by the other access methods to retrieve attributes individually by name, by functional group (font or ruler attributes, for example), and so on.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f3\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Feature Overview\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 NSAttributedString and NSMutableAttributedString add a number of features to the basic content storage of NSString:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx3360\tx3740\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	Association of arbitrary, programmer-defined attributes with ranges of characters\
·	Preservation of attribute-to-character mapping after changes (NSMutableAttributedString)\
·	Support for RTF, including file attachments and graphics\
·	Drawing in NSView objects (note that the Application Kit adds drawing methods to NSString as well)\
·	Linguistic unit (word) and line calculation\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 An attributed string identifies attributes by name, storing their values as opaque 
\b id
\b0 s in an NSDictionary. For example, the text font is stored as an NSFont object under the name given by NSFontAttributeName. You can associate any object value, by any name, with a given range of characters in the attributed string. The basic attributes defined by NSAttributedString are described below under ªAccessing Attributes.º\

\fs14 \

\fs28 A mutable attributed string keeps track of the attribute mapping as characters are added to and deleted from it and as attributes are changed. It allows you to group batches of edits with the 
\b beginEditing
\b0  and 
\b endEditing
\b0  methods, and to consolidate changes to the attribute-to-character mapping with the 
\b fix...
\b0  methods. See ªChanging a Mutable Attributed Stringº below for more information.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 An attributed string can be created from rich text (RTF) or rich text with attachments (RTFD), and can write its contents as RTF or RTFD data. Three initialization methods, 
\b initWithRTF:documentAttributes:
\b0 , 
\b initWithRTFD:documentAttributes:
\b0 , and 
\b initWithRTFDFileWrapper:documentAttributes:
\b0 , interpret rich text data. The methods for writing rich text are 
\b RTFFromRange:documentAttributes:
\b0  and 
\b RTFDFromRange:documentAttributes:
\b0 , which return rich text data for any legal range within the attributed string, and 
\b RTFDFileWrapperFromRange:documentAttributes:
\b0 , which returns the attributed string as an NSFileWrapper. NSAttributedString provides limited support for some document-level attributes, as described below under ªRTF Document Attributes.º Additional support for rich text is provided by other text-handling classes such as NSTextView. \

\fs14 \

\fs28 You can draw an attributed string in a focused NSView by invoking either the 
\b drawAtPoint:
\b0  or 
\b drawInRect:
\b0  method. Note that the Application Kit defines drawing methods for NSString as well, allowing any string object to draw itself. These methods, 
\b drawAtPoint:withAttributes:
\b0  and 
\b drawInRect:withAttributes:
\b0 , are described in the NSString Additions class specification.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 An attributed string supports the typical behavior of editors in selecting a word on a double-click with the 
\b doubleClickAtIndex:
\b0  method, and finds word breaks with 
\b nextWordFromIndex:forward:
\b0 . It also calculates line breaks with the 
\b lineBreakBeforeIndex:withinRange:
\b0  method.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f3\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Accessing Attributes\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 An attributed string identifies attributes by name, storing an 
\b id
\b0  value under the attribute name in an NSDictionary, which is in turn associated with an NSRange that indicates the characters to which the dictionary's attributes apply. You can assign any attribute name/value pair you wish to a range of characters, in addition to these standard attributes:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx6500\tx9520\f3\b\fs28\li1900\fc0\cf0 Attribute Identifier	Value Class	Default Value\

\fs6 \

\f0\b0\fs28 NSFontAttributeName	NSFont	Helvetica 12-point\
NSForegroundColorAttributeName	NSColor	black\
NSBackgroundColorAttributeName	NSColor	none (no background drawn)\
NSUnderlineStyleAttributeName	NSNumber, as an 
\b int
\b0 	none (no underline)\
NSSuperscriptAttributeName	NSNumber, as an 
\b int
\b0 	0\
NSBaselineOffsetAttributeName	NSNumber, as a 
\b float
\b0 	0.0\
NSKernAttributeName	NSNumber, as a 
\b float
\b0 	0.0\
NSLigatureAttributeName	NSNumber, as an 
\b int
\b0 	1 (standard ligatures)\
NSParagraphStyleAttributeName	NSParagraphStyle	(as returned by NSParagraphStyle's\
		
\b defaultParagraphStyle
\b0  method)\
NSAttachmentAttributeName	NSTextAttachment	none (no attachment)\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The identifiers listed are actually global NSString variables containing the attribute names. The value class is what users of an attributed string should expect the attribute values to be presented as. The default values are what they should assume if no attribute value has been explicitly set for the requested character range.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The natures of several attributes aren't obvious from name alone:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx6300\tx6580\tx15140\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	The underline attribute has only one value defined, NSSingleUnderlineStyle. All characters with this attribute value should be drawn with a single line just below the baseline.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	The superscript attribute indicates an abstract level for both super- and subscripts. The user of the attributed string can interpret this as desired, adjusting the baseline by the same or a different amount for each level, changing the font size, or both.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	The baseline offset attribute is a literal distance by which the characters should be shifted above the baseline (for positive offsets) or below (for negative offsets).\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	The kerning attribute indicates how much the following character should be shifted from its default offset as defined by the current character's font; a positive kern indicates a shift farther along and a negative kern indicates a shift closer to the current character.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	The ligature attribute determines what kinds of ligatures should be used when displaying the string. A value of 0 indicates that only ligatures essential for proper rendering of text should be used, 1 indicates that standard ligatures should be used, and 2 indicates that all available ligatures should be used. Which ligatures are standard depends on the script and possibly the font. Arabic text, for example, requires ligatures for many character sequences, but has a rich set of additional ligatures that combine characters. English text has no essential ligatures, and typically has only two standard ligatures, those for ªfiº and ªflºÐall others being considered more advanced or fancy.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 With an immutable attributed string, you assign all attributes on creating the string using methods such as 
\b initWithRTF:documentAttributes:
\b0 , which interprets attributes from the RTF data, 
\b initWithString:attributes:
\b0 , which explicitly takes an NSDictionary of name/value pairs, or 
\b initWithString:
\b0 , which assigns no attributes. See ªChanging a Mutable Attributed Stringº below for information on assigning attributes with a mutable attributed string.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 To retrieve attribute values from either type of attributed string, use any of these methods:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2320\tx3740\tx4140\f1\fs28\li2320\fc0\cf0 -
\f0 €attributesAtIndex:effectiveRange:\

\f1 -
\f0 €attributesAtIndex:longestEffectiveRange:inRange:\

\f1 -
\f0 €attribute:atIndex:effectiveRange:\

\f1 -
\f0 €attribute:atIndex:longestEffectiveRange:inRange:\

\f1 -
\f0 €fontAttributesInRange:\

\f1 -
\f0 €rulerAttributesInRange:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The first two methods return all attributes at a given index, the 
\b attribute:...
\b0  methods return the value of a single named attribute, and 
\b fontAttributesInRange:
\b0  and 
\b rulerAttributesInRange:
\b0  return attributes defined to apply only to characters or to whole paragraphs, respectively (see the individual method descriptions for more information).\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The first four methods also return by reference the 
\i effective range
\i0  and the 
\i longest effective range
\i0  of the attributes. These ranges allow you to determine the extent of attributes. Conceptually, each character in an attributed string has its own collection of attributes; however, it's often useful to know when the attributes and values are the same over a series of characters. This allows a routine to progress through an attributed string in chunks larger than a single character. In retrieving the effective range, an attributed string simply looks up information in its attribute mapping, essentially the dictionary of attributes that apply at the index requested. In retrieving the longest effective range, the attributed string continues checking characters past this basic range as long as the attribute values are the same. This extra comparison increases the execution time for these methods but guarantees a precise maximal range for the attributes requested.The code fragment below progresses through an attributed string in chunks based on the effective range. The fictitious 
\b analyzer
\b0  object here counts the number of characters in each font. The 
\b while
\b0  loop progresses as long as the effective range retrieved doesn't include the end of the attributed string, retrieving the font in effect just past the latest retrieved range. For each font attribute retrieved, 
\b analyzer
\b0  is asked to tally the number of characters in the effective range. In this example, it's possible that consecutive invocations of 
\b attribute:atIndex:effectiveRange:
\b0  will return the same value.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f4\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 NSAttributedString *attrStr;\
unsigned int length;\
NSRange effectiveRange;\
id attributeValue;\
\
length = [attrStr length];\
effectiveRange = NSMakeRange(0, 0);\
\
while (NSMaxRange(effectiveRange) < length) \{\
    attributeValue = [attrStr attribute:NSFontAttributeName\
        atIndex:NSMaxRange(effectiveRange) effectiveRange:&effectiveRange];\
    [analyzer tallyCharacterRange:effectiveRange font:attributeValue];\
\}\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 In contrast, the next code fragment progresses through the attributed string according to the maximum effective range for each font. In this case, 
\b analyzer
\b0  counts font 
\i changes
\i0 , which may not be represented by merely retrieving effective ranges. In this case the 
\b while
\b0  loop is predicated on the length of the limiting range, which begins as the entire length of the attributed string and is whittled down as the loop progresses. After 
\b analyzer
\b0  records the font change, the limit range is adjusted to account for the longest effective range retrieved.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f4\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 NSAttributedString *attrStr;\
NSRange limitRange;\
NSRange effectiveRange;\
id attributeValue;\
\
limitRange = NSMakeRange(0, [attrStr length]);\
\
while (limitRange.length > 0) \{\
    attributeValue = [attrStr attribute:NSFontAttributeName\
        atIndex:limitRange.location longestEffectiveRange:&effectiveRange\
        inRange:limitRange];\
    [analyzer recordFontChange:attributeValue];\
    limitRange = NSMakeRange(NSMaxRange(effectiveRange),\
        NSMaxRange(limitRange) - NSMaxRange(effectiveRange));\
\}\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Note that the second code fragment is more complex. Because of this, and because 
\b attribute:atIndex:longestEffectiveRange:inRange:
\b0  is somewhat slower than 
\b attribute:atIndex:effectiveRange:
\b0 , you should typically use it only when absolutely necessary for the work you're performing. In most cases working by effective range is enough.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f3\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Changing a Mutable Attributed String\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 NSMutableAttributedString declares a number of methods for changing both characters and attributes, such as the primitive 
\b replaceCharactersInRange:withString:
\b0  and 
\b setAttributes:range:
\b0 , or the more convenient methods 
\b addAttribute:value:range:
\b0 , 
\b applyFontTraits:range:
\b0 , 
\b setAlignment:range:
\b0 , and so on. All of the methods for changing a mutable attributed string properly update the mapping between characters and attributes, but after a change some inconsistencies can develop. Here are some examples of attribute consistency requirements:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx6300\tx6580\tx15140\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	Paragraph styles must apply to entire paragraphs.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Scripts may only be assigned fonts that support them. For example, Kanji and Arabic characters can't be assigned the Times-Roman font, and must be reassigned fonts that support these scripts.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Deleting attachment characters from the string requires the corresponding attachment objects to be released. Similarly, removing attachment objects requires the corresponding attachment characters to be removed from the string.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	A code editing application that displays all language keywords in boldface can automatically assign this attriute as the user changes the font or edits the text.\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 NSMutableAttributedString defines methods to fix these inconsistencies as changes are made. This allows the attributes to be cleaned up at a low level, hiding potential problems from higher levels and providing for very clean update of display as attributes change. There are six methods for fixing attributes:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2320\tx3740\tx4140\f1\fs28\li2320\fc0\cf0 -
\f0 €fixAttributesInRange:\

\f1 -
\f0 €fixAttachmentAttributeInRange:\

\f1 -
\f0 €fixFontAttributeInRange:\

\f1 -
\f0 €fixParagraphStyleAttributeInRange:\

\f1 -
\f0 €beginEditing\

\f1 -
\f0 €endEditing\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The first method, 
\b fixAttributesInRange:
\b0 , invokes the other three 
\b fix...
\b0  methods to clean up deleted attachment references, font attributes, and paragraph attributes, respectively. The individual method descriptions explain what cleanup entails for each case.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The 
\b beginEditing
\b0  and 
\b endEditing
\b0  methods are provided for subclasses of NSMutableAttributedString to override. Their default implementations do nothing. These methods allow instances of a subclass to record or buffer groups of changes and clean themselves up on receiving an 
\b endEditing
\b0  message. 
\b endEditing
\b0  also allows the receiver to notify any observers that it has been changed. NSTextStorage's implementation of 
\b endEditing
\b0 , for example, fixes changed attributes and then notifies its NSLayoutManagers that they need to re-lay and redisplay their text.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f3\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 RTF Document Attributes\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Attributed strings keep attribute information for their text only, while RTF allows for more general attributes of a document, especially regarding paper size and layout. To support higher-level objects that use attributed strings, the methods that work with RTF also read and write some RTF directives for document attributes, stored in an NSDictionary under these keys:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx6740\tx9540\f3\b\fs28\li1900\fc0\cf0 Attribute Key	Value Class\

\fs6 \

\pard\tx5480\tx9520\f0\b0\fs28\li1900\fc0\cf0 PaperSize	NSValue, as an NSSize\
LeftMargin	NSNumber, as a 
\b float\

\b0 RightMargin	NSNumber, as a 
\b float\

\b0 TopMargin	NSNumber, as a 
\b float\

\b0 BottomMargin	NSNumber, as a 
\b float\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The 
\b init
\b0  methods, such as 
\b initWithRTF:documentAttributes:
\b0 , return by reference a dictionary containing the attributes read from the RTF data, which your application can then use to set up its page layout. Similarly, RTF extraction methods such as 
\b RTFFromRange:documentAttributes:
\b0 , accept a dictionary containing those attributes and writes them into the RTF data, thus preserving the page layout information.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f3\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Attachments\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Attachments, such as embedded images or files, are represented in an attributed string by both a special character and an attribute. The character is identified by the global name NSAttachmentCharacter, and indicates the presence of an attachment at its location in the string. The attribute, identified in the string by the attribute name NSAttachmentAttributeName, is an NSTextAttachment object. An NSTextAttachment contains the data for the attachment itself, as well as an image to display when the string is drawn. You can use NSAttributedString's 
\b attributedStringWithAttachment:
\b0  class method to construct an attachment string, which you can then add to a mutable attributed string using 
\b appendAttributedString:
\b0  or 
\b insertAttributedString:atIndex:
\b0 .\

\fs14 \

